[Music]  this is your tech news briefing for  thursday august 25th i'm zoe thomas for  the wall street journal  in beijing's regulatory crackdown on  tech one of the hardest hit sectors has  been video games  what's more china's economy has been  weakening under kovitt 19 restrictions  meaning fewer people are spending money  on games and in-game purchases  so how are chinese video game makers  adapting  joining us to talk about it is our china  tech reporter shenlu  hey shenlu thanks for joining me hi zoe  thanks for having me  let's start with the situation in china  now what are video game makers facing  the video game developers large and  small have burned the brand of  regulatory crackdown for the past  year  since last year our authorities in china  have approved significantly fewer games  than before and tightened censorship of  video games and restricted play time  significantly for  young gamers and for some eight months  china's regulators didn't grant any  licenses to new games and that freeze  was lifted in april but game makers are  getting far fewer titles approved this  year  china has had some pretty tough  censorship rules for a while how is this  situation different in terms of getting  games approved so you're right china has  been curbing the video game sector since  2018  in 2018 authorities froze title approval  for some nine months  and then they froze the title approvals  again in 2021 the restrictions were  lifted  in april but video game makers we talked  to for the story told us that now the  timeline for getting title approval is  much longer  which means after investing a lot of  money in making video games and  operating them and buying server storage  they can't charge users until they get  their title approval so when the  application is stuck in the queue they  can't make money off of their games  what are authorities looking for are  there specific things that the the  sensors are objecting to  that's a billion dollar question  everybody wants to know the video game  developers certainly want to know why  the authorities are delaying approving  and what they are actually looking for  in the video game content people we've  talked to told us that the process has  largely been non-transparent  but late last year executives at video  game developers participated in a  workshop where authorities told them  certain contents were not allowed in  video game content and those include  same-sex relationships transgender  identities or  what authorities deemed fabricated  historical events  i know another challenge that they're  facing that we've talked about on this  show before is the limit of play time  that china has placed on young gamers  those under 18. you know so given all of  these challenges chinese video game  makers are starting to look overseas how  were they doing that  so so far video game developers big and  small are looking for opportunities  overseas for example tencent the world's  largest video game maker  has doubled down on buying and joining  with foreign studios this year not ease  china's second largest video game  developer has opened two new studios  abroad at least this year and less known  chinese video game publishers like  mihoyo and litusgames have also  established their global publishing  labels to cater to an international  audience overseas so it's a mix of  acquiring studios and partnering with  studios or buying ips and establishing  their own global headquarters and  publishing labels  how big of a shift is this i mean is  this chinese video game company saying  you know we're just too big for our  current market or is this a full  recalibration of their current business  structure and their business strategy  large companies are more concerned about  future growth opportunities so they're  actively exploring new growth  opportunities overseas as we see  the number of game players and the  revenue from the chinese games market  both  declined in the first half of 2022 but  the revenue from overseas has grown so  this is one of the main reasons why  chinese companies are actively  accelerating their  expansion overseas but for smaller  companies the tighter regulation which  means chinese regulators are approving  fewer  new titles each year now is detrimental  to the smaller companies businesses  because they are waiting for the  approval of new games to be able to  monetize the games so for a lot of video  game developers there's a new urgency  around expanding their businesses  overseas because many think that they if  they don't expand to overseas markets  they're gonna die in the domestic market  how are chinese regulators reacting to  this shift overseas are they concerned  at all about chinese game makers uh  starting to sell more overseas  so far we haven't seen any sign of  chinese regulators being concerned about  video game developers expanding overseas  in fact  they are encouraging video game  developers and other content makers to  sort of export their content and the  chinese culture overseas because that's  um because china also wants to exert its  soft power overseas and video game is a  great venue for that  so i wonder what this means then for  video game players and and for the  company's kind of long-term outlooks is  this going to change kind of how they  see themselves going forward  yeah so several large video game makers  have said they're going to focus on  global players instead of focusing on  just one geographic region  and that is particularly its ceo has  said that they want their  international game revenue to account  for about half of its total game revenue  which is a significant jump from the  current 10 percent so that tells us  about how overseas market is important  for  chinese video game makers now  all right that's our china tech reporter  shen liu chan lu thanks so much for  joining us thank you so much for having  me zoe  and that's today's tech news briefing if  you're looking for more tech stories  head over to wsj.com and if you enjoy  this show how about leaving us a  five-star rating and a review wherever  you get your podcasts i'm zoe thomas for  the wall street journal  thanks for listening  [Music]  you

